## Solution

**Setup.** Recall the general construction of the free category $\mathbf{Free}(G)$ generated by a directed graph $G$:

- **Objects** are the vertices of $G$.
- **Morphisms** from $x$ to $y$ are finite *paths* from $x$ to $y$, i.e. sequences $e_1 e_2 \cdots e_n$ of edges with $\mathrm{src}(e_1)=x$, $\mathrm{tgt}(e_n)=y$, and $\mathrm{tgt}(e_i)=\mathrm{src}(e_{i+1})$ for $i=1,\dots,n-1$. For each vertex $x$ there is also the *empty path* $\mathrm{id}_x$ (a path of length $0$).
- **Composition** of paths is concatenation: $(e_1\cdots e_n)\circ(f_1\cdots f_m)=f_1\cdots f_m\, e_1\cdots e_n$ when the target of the first matches the source of the second.
- **Identities** are the empty paths, which act as units under concatenation.

The universal property: any graph homomorphism from $G$ into the underlying graph of a category $\mathcal C$ extends uniquely to a functor $\mathbf{Free}(G)\to\mathcal C$.

**The specific graph.** Let $G$ be the digraph with
- one vertex, call it $\star$;
- two distinct loop edges $a:\star\to\star$ and $b:\star\to\star$.

**Objects.** Since $G$ has a single vertex, $\mathbf{Free}(G)$ has a single object, namely $\star$.

**Morphisms.** A path in $G$ is a finite sequence of edges $e_1 e_2\cdots e_n$ where each $e_i\in\{a,b\}$ and the composability condition $\mathrm{tgt}(e_i)=\mathrm{src}(e_{i+1})$ holds. Because every edge is a loop on $\star$, we have $\mathrm{tgt}(e_i)=\star=\mathrm{src}(e_{i+1})$ automatically for all $i$. Hence **every** finite word over the alphabet $\{a,b\}$ is a valid path, and conversely every path is such a word. Including the empty path $\varepsilon$ (the identity at $\star$), the hom-set is

$$\mathrm{Hom}(\star,\star)=\{a,b\}^{*}=\bigl\{\varepsilon,\; a,\; b,\; aa,\; ab,\; ba,\; bb,\; aaa,\; aab,\;\dots\bigr\},$$

the set of all finite words (including the empty word) on two letters.

**Composition.** With only one object, every pair of morphisms is composable. Composition is concatenation of words:

$$w\circ v = v\,w \qquad\text{(or }w\,v\text{, depending on diagrammatic order convention),}$$

i.e. the word obtained by writing the two words one after another. This is associative because concatenation of strings is associative, and $\varepsilon$ is a two-sided unit because concatenating with the empty word changes nothing.

**Structure.** Thus $\mathbf{Free}(G)$ is a **one-object category** whose single hom-set $\{a,b\}^{*}$ forms a monoid under concatenation — specifically the **free monoid on two generators** $a$ and $b$. Equivalently, it is the free category on one object and two generating endomorphisms.

**Universal property (concretized).** For any category $\mathcal C$ and any choice of an object $X\in\mathcal C$ together with two endomorphisms $f,g\in\mathrm{End}_{\mathcal C}(X)$, there is a unique functor $F:\mathbf{Free}(G)\to\mathcal C$ with $F(\star)=X$, $F(a)=f$, $F(b)=g$. Concretely, $F$ sends a word $w=e_1\cdots e_n$ (each $e_i\in\{a,b\}$) to the composite $F(e_1)\circ\cdots\circ F(e_n)$, i.e. to the corresponding product of $f$'s and $g$'s in $\mathrm{End}_{\mathcal C}(X)$, and sends $\varepsilon$ to $\mathrm{id}_X$. Uniqueness follows because every morphism in $\mathbf{Free}(G)$ is a word in $a,b$, so a functor is determined by its values on the generators $a$ and $b$.

**Summary.** The free category generated by the digraph with one vertex and two distinct loops is the **free monoid on two generators, regarded as a one-object category**: it has a single object $\star$; its morphisms are all finite words over the two-letter alphabet $\{a,b\}$ (including the empty word $\varepsilon$ as the identity); composition is word concatenation; and it satisfies the universal property that choosing any object with two endomorphisms in any category determines a unique functor out of it.

